WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.800
I'm exploring the fascinating world of plants, from the most bizarre to the most beautiful.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:31.300
I will be using today's latest technology to reveal a whole new dimension in the lives of plants.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:43.800
I'll trace them from their beginnings on land to their vital place in nature today.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:59.800
And discover a microscopic world that is invisible to the naked eye.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:08.800
We will move from our time scale to theirs.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:16.800
We will explore the extraordinary ways by which some plants survive in the harshest conditions.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.800
And we will do all this in one unique place.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:26.800
A microcosm of the whole plant world.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:48.800
The Royal Botanic Gardens at Kew.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.800
I've seen plants growing in their natural habitats all over the world.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:02:00.800
But here in Kew it's possible to examine them in a way that is impossible in the wild.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:07.800
Here some 90% of all known plant species are represented in one form or another.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:25.800
So in this one place you can survey the entire plant kingdom.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:31.800
Plants flourish most dramatically in places where there is a lot of warmth and a lot of water.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.800
The wet zone.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:46.800
So here we can discover how plants first established themselves on land to become the very foundation of all terrestrial life.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.800
Rainforests occupy only about 2% of the world's land surface.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.800
But they contain over 50% of the world's species.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.800
And many of these wet zone plants have extraordinary survival strategies.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:11.800
To study them Kew has built a rainforest here on the banks of the Thames.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.800
This is a rainforest like no other.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:29.800
The Palm House was constructed in 1844 from over 200 tons of iron and 16,000 panes of glass.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.800
No one had ever built a glass house on this scale before.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.800
And to do so the architects borrowed techniques from the ship building industry.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:45.800
Which may explain why the Palm House looks like the upturned hull of a ship.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:56.800
Its purpose was to provide a home for the tropical plants that Victorian explorers brought back from their adventures in the tropics.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:04.800
It was an engineering wonder of the age.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:12.800
And it is still a Britannical wonder.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:18.800
Most of the plants at this end of the house are kept in pots as they would have been in Victorian times.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.800
And this is almost certainly the oldest pot plant in the world.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:37.800
This is Encephalatos altinstiniae and it came here in 1775.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:49.800
Altinstiniae has since been joined by a multitude of other wet zone plants.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.800
Together they constitute a unique global rainforest.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:05:05.800
A living laboratory where we can watch and observe the fascinating ways in which rainforest plants interact and behave.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:10.800
Conditions in this glass house are near perfect for wet zone plants.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.800
It's warm, it's humid, there's plenty of light.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:25.800
And as a consequence plants grow very rapidly and all through the year.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:33.800
If you speed up time plants begin to reveal their true nature.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:45.800
They're not passive organisms as you might think, but competitive creatures every bit as aggressive as animals.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:51.800
They're locked in a desperate battle for light and space.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:06:01.800
They stretch and pulse as they strive to barge their way into pole position.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:23.800
Creepers and vines reach around for the branch or stem of another plant on which to hitch a ride.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:38.800
Bamboos, a family of giant grasses, are capable of extraordinary speeds as they race towards the brightest light at the top of the canopy.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:45.800
They're the fastest growing of all plants.

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:52.800
Some species can grow a whole meter in a single day.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:07:14.800
And at 30 meters tall, as high as a nine-story office block, they can compete with the tallest rainforest trees.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:23.800
The one resource wet zone plants don't need to fight for is water.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:37.800
In fact, there can be so much rain in the rainforest that some species have to have special ways of getting rid of it.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:46.800
This is the taro plant, and it has the most extraordinary leaves.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:55.800
Watch what happens when I pour water on them.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:02.800
The water rolls, leaving the leaf behind almost dry.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:11.800
And that's because the leaf is covered with very, very tiny microscopic structures which hold aloft the droplets of water.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:18.800
And as a consequence, only about 2% of water touches the actual leaf surface itself.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:24.800
And as the droplets roll away, they carry with them the dirt and bacteria.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:36.800
So in effect, these leaves are self-cleaning.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:47.800
But there are some plants that use water in a different way, and they live high up in the canopy.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:54.800
A tank bromeliad, a plant that is dispensed with the need of growing in the soil on the ground,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:01.800
and instead attaches itself to branches up here in the canopy where there's plenty of light.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:15.800
And since it's so far from the ground, it's had to develop ways of getting its nutriment in a quite different way.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:26.800
They collect some of the rain that every day drenches the South American rainforest where they live.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:37.800
Their leaves, which are very broad, channel the rainwater into a central reservoir.

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:45.800
At their base, the leaves are so tightly pressed together that the tank they create is watertight.

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:51.800
Some species can hold up to 50 litres of liquid.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:10:03.800
The water not only hydrates the plant, but it also provides a home for a lot of creatures that wouldn't otherwise be able to live up here.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:09.800
These little wrigglers are the larvae of mosquitoes.

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:18.800
In the wild, there are lots of different creatures that can be found in these little pools.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:32.800
This is Ranitomia imitator, a poison-dark frog from Peru.

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:42.800
It's the size of a thumbnail and the only monogamous amphibian in the world.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:55.800
Some individuals spend their whole lives, from tadpole to adult, in and around bromeliad pools.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:11:01.800
And high in the canopy, such a pool provides an excellent home.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:09.800
It's relatively safe and there's a ready supply of food.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:22.800
The plant benefits from the arrangement because the droppings produced by its lodger are a nutritious fertiliser.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:34.800
The bromeliads and the frogs that live in them are only one example of the complex relationships that abound in the rainforests.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:44.800
Here, the lives of each and every living thing, both animal and plant, are intimately entwined.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:51.800
It's a tangled web that has evolved over many millions of years.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:12:00.800
Throughout their history, rainforests have provided plants with a refuge against harsher conditions elsewhere in the planet.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:05.800
So in rainforests, you find some very ancient plant families.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:23.800
This saikhan, for example, belongs to a family that certainly provided food for the dinosaurs.

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:43.800
But the rainforest is such a rich environment that plants from all stages in their history can still be found here.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:58.800
The first were slimy, thread-like algae that emerged from rivers and swamps to grow on the wet, muddy margins.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:13.800
40 million years or so later, some developed watertight coverings that enabled them to move onto drier, though still moist, land.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:30.800
They were the liverworts and the mosses.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:41.800
Later still, some of the descendants of those plants stiffened their stems so that they were able to stand upright as they reached up towards the light.

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:53.800
They were the ferns and horsetails.

00:13:53.800 --> 00:14:12.800
This greening of the earth changed the course of life, allowing animals to follow plants out of permanent water.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:18.800
As they spread across the land, the plants pumped out oxygen from their leaves.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:30.800
So from the very beginning, land animals were dependent upon plants not only for food, but for the very air they breathe.

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:37.800
A major difficulty for plants in their new environment was reproduction.

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:47.800
If they were to produce fertile seeds, pollen from one had to reach and fertilize the over the eggs of another.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:55.800
They produced a lot of polymers, pine, yues and firs, then as now used wind.

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:06.800
They produced vast numbers of pollen grains, scarcely bigger than particles of dust, which the wind could carry for hundreds of miles.

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:11.800
The technique was successful, but very wasteful.

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:13.720
The number of

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:21.800
polymers had to be produced if just one was to reach its target.

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:32.800
But then, about 140 million years ago, some developed a much more efficient way of doing that.

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:39.800
With flowers.

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:49.800
How this happened was for a long time a total mystery to scientists, including one of the greatest, Darwin.

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:56.800
Charles Darwin was baffled by what he called an abominable mystery.

00:15:56.800 --> 00:16:07.800
The fact that halfway through the age of the dinosaurs, flowering plants suddenly produced a vast number of species and within a very short period of time.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:15.800
That contradicted one of the characteristics of evolution as Darwin saw it, that it was a slow and gradual process.

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:18.800
How could that have happened?

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:30.800
From the moment Darwin posed the question, scientists have been striving for an answer.

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:42.800
Recent fossil discoveries and modern DNA analysis of species present and past have transformed our understanding of this fascinating period.

00:16:42.800 --> 00:17:01.800
Using that information, scientists with work pioneered here in Kew have been able to construct the ancestral tree of the whole of plant life.

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:11.800
It begins with algae, the simplest of the plants, which are followed by the mosses.

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:22.800
The thickness of the rising branches indicates the relative number of species in each individual group.

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:33.800
For the first half of the plant's evolutionary history, all the branches of plant life flourished roughly equally.

00:17:33.800 --> 00:17:49.800
But then, 140 million years ago, there was an explosive radiation of species, Darwin's mystery.

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:59.800
These were the angiosperms, flowering plants.

00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:13.800
Their evolution was more gradual than Darwin had thought, but there is no question that the angiosperms quickly became the dominant group.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:24.800
They diversified into a huge range of species, which have occupied almost every known habitat on Earth.

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:29.800
But what happened to stimulate this dramatic radiation?

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:33.800
There was a happy coincidence of two events.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:44.800
The first was a doubling in the genetic material of plants, allowing them to evolve more quickly when their environment changed.

00:18:44.800 --> 00:19:00.800
The second was one such change, the development of a unique relationship between plants and animals.

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:14.800
The effect of this coincidence can be seen in one of the very first flowering plants to evolve.

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:20.800
This is the Waterlily House, the hottest and most humid environment in Kew.

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:29.800
It was built in 1852 to accommodate the latest botanical discovery, the giant Amazon Waterlily.

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:48.800
Now it's February and the pond is almost bare, but in a few weeks, exotic Waterlilys of all kinds will be sprouting.

00:19:48.800 --> 00:20:00.800
The ancestors of today's Waterlilys, as we know from their fossils, were among the first plants to produce flowers.

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:30.800
Bright petals, modified leaves, were an advertisement to flying insects.

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:34.800
They signaled the presence of highly nutritious pollen.

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:44.800
The shape of the flower worked like a primitive trap, forcing the insects to stumble about and bump into the plant's reproductive structures.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:53.800
In doing so, the insects transferred onto the Waterlily pollen which they had accidentally collected on earlier visits to other flowers.

00:20:53.800 --> 00:21:04.800
So now plants could trick insects into transporting their pollen directly from one plant to another with a door-to-door service.

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:20.800
The flower of the giant Amazon Waterlily can close totally, holding the insects captive for several hours, thus making absolutely sure that pollination occurs.

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:31.800
Many different species of plant followed suit, each evolving its own particular flowers to attract its insect messengers.

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:38.800
Competition for their services drove the plants to diversify.

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:57.800
Insects favored petals that were brighter, scents that was more perfumed, and flowers that had the sweetest nectar.

00:21:57.800 --> 00:22:08.800
The appearance of such temptations had a huge effect on the insects.

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:21.800
They too began to diversify. A multitude of forms could better harness the potential of the numerous species of flower.

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:28.800
Insects with large eyes could spot the flowers.

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:36.800
Powerful wings could carry them between plants.

00:22:36.800 --> 00:22:51.800
And complex mouthparts could delve into the deepest nectare.

00:22:51.800 --> 00:23:01.800
So plants and insects evolved together, driving their mutual diversity.

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:11.800
Rainforests are the culmination of this process, containing a greater variety of species than any other habitat.

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:18.800
And here you can see that some plants didn't restrict themselves to insects.

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:26.800
This, for example, is known as the jade vine. But why is it this extraordinarily mesmerizing blue-green color?

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:35.800
Well, we know that it's fertilized by bats. That's why the flowers hang out in the open so the bats can get at them quite easily.

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:55.800
And so maybe this color stands out particularly boldly as far as bats are concerned in the moonlight.

00:23:55.800 --> 00:24:00.800
When the bat arrives, it sticks its head into the flower to get at the honey.

00:24:00.800 --> 00:24:09.800
And as it does so and presses there, out from this hook, come at the stamens and dab pollen on its back.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:17.800
So when the bat goes away, it takes the pollen to another flower.

00:24:17.800 --> 00:24:27.800
Each and every species of flowering plant has its own unique evolutionary story that's closely coupled with the animals that pollinate it.

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:35.800
But one family of flowering plant has developed this relationship in more complex ways than any other.

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:45.800
And in doing so has become the most numerous and diverse on the planet.

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:58.800
There are an estimated 25,000 species of orchid.

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:07.800
This family had a particular fascination for Charles Darwin as he reveals in one of his letters that's kept here at Kew.

00:25:07.800 --> 00:25:14.800
He says, I have been extremely much interested with catecedum. That's an orchid.

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:24.800
And indeed, with many exotic orchids, orchids have interested me as much as almost anything in my life.

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:29.800
Charles Darwin.

00:25:29.800 --> 00:25:40.800
One species of these amazing plants can be found growing outside in the grounds of Kew.

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:52.800
Orchids are extraordinary plants from many points of view, but one of them is that the lower lip of the flower is controlled by a special set of genes.

00:25:52.800 --> 00:26:02.800
So that means that it can evolve and change its shape and color while the rest of its petals remain the same.

00:26:02.800 --> 00:26:08.800
The lower lip of this little orchid has evolved to look roughly like a bee.

00:26:08.800 --> 00:26:17.800
And people used to think that was a kind of warning to warn cows not to eat it on the grounds that they wouldn't want to get stung on the tongue.

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:26.800
But now we know that's not the case. This is a mimic of a female bee that's attracting a male to mate it.

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:30.800
And when the male mates, it will pollinate the flower.

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:45.800
How do we know that's true? Because this little flower produces the scent which is exactly the same as that of a female bee trying to attract a mate.

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:54.800
The unique genetic makeup of orchids has allowed them to evolve an almost unbelievable degree of complexity.

00:26:54.800 --> 00:27:04.800
And they produce their greatest variety and complexity in the wet zone.

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:13.800
At Kew, they're cultivated inside the Princess of Wales Conservatory.

00:27:13.800 --> 00:27:42.800
In this section, conditions are perfect for them.

00:27:42.800 --> 00:28:01.800
Each orchid species has its own characteristic form and color.

00:28:01.800 --> 00:28:24.800
They represent the pinnacle of evolutionary cooperation between animals and plants.

00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:42.800
In their most extreme form, the relationship for the plant is an exclusive one. Only one species of insect will have the right equipment to claim the plant's nectar.

00:28:42.800 --> 00:28:52.800
This relationship between a particular kind of insect and a particular kind of plant produces some extraordinary results.

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:59.800
This, for example, is an orchid called Catacetum.

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:06.800
Unusually for orchids, some plants are male and others are female.

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:11.800
This is a male.

00:29:11.800 --> 00:29:20.800
It produces a kind of scent that attracts just one species of small, glossy, beautifully colored bee.

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:30.800
The bee lands on the lip of the orchid and thrusts its head into the orchid's flower itself.

00:29:30.800 --> 00:29:45.800
And that touches a trigger and sticks onto the bee's back this extraordinary thing, which is in fact a bundle of pollen grains called pollinium.

00:29:45.800 --> 00:30:00.800
This has a little cap on it, which after a minute or so falls off and reveals there, that little horseshoe shaped bundle of pollen grains.

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:05.800
High speed cameras can show us the trigger mechanism.

00:30:05.800 --> 00:30:11.800
The pollinium accelerates with great force.

00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:17.800
It also ensures that it sticks firmly onto the insect's back.

00:30:17.800 --> 00:30:24.800
The bee, no doubt somewhat dazed, flies away and maybe thinks it's not going to do that again,

00:30:24.800 --> 00:30:33.800
but is nonetheless attracted to another rather different looking flower, which is the female, but which produces just that sort of scent.

00:30:33.800 --> 00:30:43.800
And it sticks its head into the female flower and this little bundle of pollen, like a key, fits into a little aperture, like a lock.

00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:49.800
And it pulls off the pollen and leaves on the bee's back a little bundle.

00:30:49.800 --> 00:30:58.800
And lo and behold, pollination has been achieved.

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:07.800
It's hard to imagine how evolution produced such a complex pollination mechanism.

00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:14.800
But there is one orchid whose life story is even more astonishing.

00:31:14.800 --> 00:31:23.800
Many flowers produce a sweet nectar to entice insects and other animals to come and pollinate them.

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:40.800
But this orchid from Madagascar, the comet orchid, carries its nectar at the end of extremely long spurs from the back of its flower.

00:31:40.800 --> 00:31:46.800
What on earth could have a tongue long enough to reach down those huge long spurs?

00:31:46.800 --> 00:31:54.800
Charles Darwin, who studied the fertilization of orchids, decided it could only be a moth. But nobody had ever seen it.

00:31:54.800 --> 00:32:08.800
Until some years after his death, he was proved right.

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:14.800
This is Xanthopan Morgani predictor.

00:32:14.800 --> 00:32:22.800
Morgan's predicted sphinx moth.

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:32.800
With special night vision cameras, we can show why such an immensely long tongue is needed.

00:32:32.800 --> 00:33:01.800
It's a third of a meter long. Exactly the same length as the spur beneath the flower.

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:07.800
The relationship between orchids and their insect pollinators is certainly very intimate.

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:21.800
But the connection between these passion flowers and butterflies is even more complex.

00:33:21.800 --> 00:33:34.800
This is Passiflora, the passion vine.

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:45.800
Like orchids, its brightly colored displays attract pollinators.

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:51.800
One of them is a heliconid butterfly.

00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:57.800
But this relationship between an insect and a plant is no straightforward trade-off.

00:33:57.800 --> 00:34:07.800
Passion vine and butterfly have been engaged in an ongoing game of one-upmanship for many millions of years.

00:34:07.800 --> 00:34:13.800
The butterfly doesn't just want nectar.

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:22.800
It also wants a place to lay its eggs.

00:34:22.800 --> 00:34:30.800
A place where its caterpillars will have something good to eat immediately nearby.

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:39.800
Passion flower leaves.

00:34:39.800 --> 00:35:01.800
Its lungs have huge appetites.

00:35:01.800 --> 00:35:06.800
But some passion vines have fought back.

00:35:06.800 --> 00:35:11.800
They've solved a way to protect themselves.

00:35:11.800 --> 00:35:19.800
Poison in their leaves.

00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:26.800
But sometimes even this is no defense.

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:34.800
Some caterpillars not only tolerate the leaves' toxins, but store them in their flesh.

00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:47.800
Those toxins serve as a defense against the caterpillar's predators.

00:35:47.800 --> 00:35:53.800
And the story doesn't even end here.

00:35:53.800 --> 00:35:59.800
The passion vine has evolved a second line of defense.

00:35:59.800 --> 00:36:05.800
This one has leaves that give the impression of being a swarm of butterflies.

00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:16.800
By mimicking the real ones, it may be suggesting that there is no perching room for others.

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:22.800
And in the detail of their leaves, you can see something even more surprising.

00:36:22.800 --> 00:36:32.800
This kind of passion flower has a special way of dissuading female butterflies from laying eggs on its leaves.

00:36:32.800 --> 00:36:42.800
It imitates eggs with these little yellow spots so that female butterflies will think that these leaves are already taken, as you might say.

00:36:42.800 --> 00:37:00.800
And this different species of passion flower does the same thing, but imitates eggs in a different way, with tiny little growths at the base of the leaf.

00:37:00.800 --> 00:37:11.800
The passion flower's tactics illustrate some of the complex ways by which plants dissuade animals from eating them.

00:37:11.800 --> 00:37:20.800
But some wet zone species have turned the tables.

00:37:20.800 --> 00:37:27.800
This bud will soon become a leaf.

00:37:27.800 --> 00:37:35.800
It's no ordinary leaf.

00:37:35.800 --> 00:37:44.800
It has a special, altogether more sinister purpose.

00:37:44.800 --> 00:37:52.800
This is nepenthes, the pitcher plant.

00:37:52.800 --> 00:38:04.800
It grows in nutrient-poor soils, so has to find nitrogen and minerals in another way.

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:14.800
The leaf, just like a flower, attracts insects with a reward.

00:38:14.800 --> 00:38:22.800
The pitcher is colored and scented to appeal to flies looking for a meal of rotting flesh.

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:32.800
The visitors are rewarded with a greasy substance on the underside of the pitcher's lid.

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:42.800
They want something in return. Not pollen, but a meal.

00:38:42.800 --> 00:38:53.800
The lip of the pitcher is covered in tiny, slippery ridges.

00:38:53.800 --> 00:39:00.800
Wax lubricates the surface further.

00:39:00.800 --> 00:39:14.800
It's extremely difficult to hold on, even for a fly.

00:39:14.800 --> 00:39:21.800
Once inside, there's no escape.

00:39:21.800 --> 00:39:28.800
The leaf holds a pool of digestive liquid.

00:39:28.800 --> 00:39:35.800
This contains microscopic elastic filaments which give it the properties of quicksand.

00:39:35.800 --> 00:39:41.800
The more the insect struggles, the deeper it sinks.

00:39:41.800 --> 00:39:55.800
Enzymes begin to dissolve the victim's body while it's still alive.

00:39:55.800 --> 00:40:02.800
Some pitchers aren't content with just insects. This one eats mice.

00:40:02.800 --> 00:40:06.800
The mice come along, perhaps attracted by the sweet nectar on the lip.

00:40:06.800 --> 00:40:11.800
They fall in, they can't get a purchase to get out, they drown,

00:40:11.800 --> 00:40:16.800
and eventually the enzymes in the pitcher's fluid dissolves the body,

00:40:16.800 --> 00:40:27.800
and eventually there's nothing left but a bit of fur and bones.

00:40:27.800 --> 00:40:35.800
This pitcher is called Nepenthes loei.

00:40:35.800 --> 00:40:40.800
It lives on the forested slopes of Borneo's mountains,

00:40:40.800 --> 00:40:45.800
and it's perhaps the most extraordinary pitcher of them all.

00:40:45.800 --> 00:40:51.800
The evidence is that it too attracts small mammals.

00:40:51.800 --> 00:40:56.800
It secretes a sort of nectar from the underside of the lid there,

00:40:56.800 --> 00:41:03.800
and that attracts little tree shrews.

00:41:03.800 --> 00:41:11.800
This engaging little animal is Tupaya montana, the mountain tree shrew.

00:41:11.800 --> 00:41:17.800
It feeds on fruit and any insects it can find.

00:41:17.800 --> 00:41:26.800
It also visits pitcher plants.

00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:34.800
Until recently, what happens next was a mystery.

00:41:34.800 --> 00:41:43.800
This footage seems to show that the animal has found a way of evading the slippery death trap.

00:41:43.800 --> 00:41:54.800
What is more, it's feeding on the underside of the pitcher's lid.

00:41:54.800 --> 00:41:59.800
This pitcher doesn't get its nutriment from the bodies of dead animals.

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:04.800
It's got another way of sustaining itself.

00:42:04.800 --> 00:42:07.800
The tree shrews come to lick it, and when they do,

00:42:07.800 --> 00:42:13.800
their rear end is directly over the pitcher so that their droppings fall into it.

00:42:13.800 --> 00:42:22.800
And it's that that provides this plant with its nourishment.

00:42:22.800 --> 00:42:30.800
It's even been suggested that that nectar contains a laxative to persuade the tree shrew to do just that.

00:42:53.800 --> 00:42:59.800
The stability of the rainforest environment over time has allowed the coevolution of animals and plants

00:42:59.800 --> 00:43:04.800
to develop to an unrivaled degree of complexity.

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:10.800
But with complexity comes fragility.

00:43:10.800 --> 00:43:19.800
Each and every species has its own place in the complex working of the rainforest.

00:43:19.800 --> 00:43:38.800
In recent years, Kew's unique position as a living laboratory of the wet zone has become more important than ever.

00:43:38.800 --> 00:43:42.800
These days, Kew's role extends far beyond these 300 acres,

00:43:42.800 --> 00:43:51.800
and here people are working to rescue extremely rare plants from total extinction.

00:43:51.800 --> 00:43:59.800
The danger of losing a single species is taken very seriously indeed.

00:43:59.800 --> 00:44:09.800
Here in the waterlily room, experts face a desperate scramble to save one species from oblivion.

00:44:09.800 --> 00:44:16.800
Nestling amongst the giant waterlily pads is the tiny Nymphaeothermarum,

00:44:16.800 --> 00:44:26.800
the Rwandan waterlily, the smallest and rarest waterlily in the world.

00:44:26.800 --> 00:44:35.800
Extinct in the wild, these precious few individuals are some of the last remaining specimens.

00:44:35.800 --> 00:44:45.800
These photographs show its only known native habitat, as it was over 20 years ago, a hot spring in Rwanda.

00:44:45.800 --> 00:44:55.800
It was destroyed just recently when locals redirected its waters to supply a laundry.

00:44:55.800 --> 00:45:02.800
A single specimen was brought back to Europe, but the species remained on the brink.

00:45:02.800 --> 00:45:09.800
Its seeds would germinate, but the seedlings always died.

00:45:09.800 --> 00:45:17.800
Then some seeds were given to this man at Kew, Carlos Magdalena.

00:45:17.800 --> 00:45:28.800
Known in Spain as the plant messiah, Carlos is famous for his great skill in rescuing endangered species.

00:45:28.800 --> 00:45:35.800
But even Carlos nearly met his match with this tiny plant.

00:45:35.800 --> 00:45:39.800
I tested many different seedlings, nothing seemed to work.

00:45:39.800 --> 00:45:43.800
Well that meant that the species was about to disappear forever.

00:45:43.800 --> 00:45:48.800
Exactly, and that was very worrying for me, so I started becoming a little bit obsessed with it.

00:45:48.800 --> 00:46:02.800
It's just a bit distressing to know that something that you can do or not make a difference to a species.

00:46:02.800 --> 00:46:09.800
Carlos didn't give up. He had one more unlikely idea.

00:46:09.800 --> 00:46:17.800
He tried growing the waterlily out of water.

00:46:17.800 --> 00:46:24.800
To think about it, growing a waterlily out of the water is something as crazy as trying to grow a cactus floating in a pond.

00:46:24.800 --> 00:46:32.800
So it was the last unlikely thing that logically you will do, and then that is the result.

00:46:32.800 --> 00:46:38.800
Carlos's stroke of genius was to make use of this plant's strange ecological quirk.

00:46:38.800 --> 00:46:46.800
Nearly all other waterlily is only grown in deep water, but the Rwandan Spring was very shallow, little more than damp mud.

00:46:46.800 --> 00:46:54.800
And so by growing it in pots above the water, Carlos replicated its natural habitat.

00:46:54.800 --> 00:47:02.800
Within a few short weeks, there were 50 specimens all set to flower.

00:47:02.800 --> 00:47:13.800
So this is a crucial moment because if this works and it's clearly going to, you have in fact yourself saved the species.

00:47:13.800 --> 00:47:30.800
If I didn't have this crazy idea, the whole species will be gone forever.

00:47:30.800 --> 00:47:57.800
That's absolutely charming. Beautiful.

00:47:57.800 --> 00:48:09.800
In an environment with so many species and so many spectacular ones, it might seem odd to spend so much trouble in trying to save just one comparatively inconspicuous one.

00:48:09.800 --> 00:48:20.800
But the relationships within the rainforest are so complex and so extensive that the loss of just one can have a whole series of unpredictable consequences.

00:48:20.800 --> 00:48:28.800
The loss of a plant can mean the loss of an insect. The loss of an insect can mean that a bush loses its pollinator.

00:48:28.800 --> 00:48:32.800
The loss of a bush can mean that a mammal has lost its food plant.

00:48:32.800 --> 00:48:37.800
Nobody can say exactly when or if such things are going to happen.

00:48:37.800 --> 00:48:49.800
But to me, preventing it happening in the first place seems to make absolute sense.

00:48:49.800 --> 00:48:56.800
The story of plants spreads well beyond the wet zone.

00:48:56.800 --> 00:49:04.800
They have evolved to occupy almost every environment on the planet.

00:49:04.800 --> 00:49:11.800
They survive in regions of constant change.

00:49:11.800 --> 00:49:19.800
They thrive in soils that almost never see rain.

00:49:19.800 --> 00:49:42.800
And as we will discover, they do much of their living in ways that go almost entirely undetected by us.
